1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:10,820
This video shows how to put an animation from Pixodesk 2D Animator on a web page, as a dot Lottie file. It takes one package, one canvas and a few lines of script.

2
00:00:12,067 --> 00:00:13,947
Let's start with the page as it is.

3
00:00:18,067 --> 00:00:20,418
A heading, and nothing that moves yet.

4
00:00:21,667 --> 00:00:27,753
To play dot Lottie files, the page needs the dot Lottie player. Let's install it.

5
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:37,516
The dot Lottie web player is in, one package.

6
00:00:38,733 --> 00:00:44,637
Now for the animation itself. Let's switch to the editor, where a football is already drawn.

7
00:00:46,167 --> 00:00:48,962
It's a vector drawing, standing still.

8
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:53,335
To make it move, let's switch on Auto mode.

9
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:59,412
With Auto mode on, every change becomes a keyframe.

10
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:03,925
Half a second in, let's drag the ball down to the floor.

11
00:01:07,067 --> 00:01:10,802
A keyframe appears on the timeline, holding the new position.

12
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:13,966
At one second, let's bring it back up.

13
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:20,800
Now it falls and rises again, in one second.

14
00:01:22,033 --> 00:01:28,982
A real ball speeds up as it falls, and slows down as it rises. Let's set that easing.

15
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:40,750
Gravity easing, falling and rising, makes the bounce feel natural.

16
00:01:41,967 --> 00:01:47,452
The animation is ready. Let's save it as a dot Lottie file, into the public folder.

17
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,753
Dot Lottie is the compact, zipped form of a Lottie file.

18
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,750
Now, back to our page.

19
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:05,244
The animation file is already there, in the public folder.

20
00:02:06,467 --> 00:02:09,549
Let's add a canvas to the page, for the player to draw on.

21
00:02:15,967 --> 00:02:18,997
This player draws on a canvas, not with SVG.

22
00:02:20,233 --> 00:02:23,734
Then, in the script, let's import the player.

23
00:02:30,167 --> 00:02:32,465
It comes from the dot Lottie web package.

24
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:36,887
And start it on the canvas, with the animation file.

25
00:02:49,533 --> 00:02:54,235
It takes the canvas, the file's address, and loop and autoplay.

26
00:02:55,467 --> 00:02:57,765
Let's reload the page, and watch.

27
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:06,897
There it is. The ball bounces, drawn on the canvas.

28
00:03:08,933 --> 00:03:19,226
So, one package, one dot Lottie file saved from the editor, a canvas, and a few lines of script. That's all it takes to bring an animation onto a web page.
